Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOLI Center, EDSA corner Quezon Avenue, West Triangle, Quezon City S. 2DL HON. LIGAYA H. HERALDO Acting Vice•vlayor Vinzons, Camarines Norte Dear Hon. Heraldo: http: l/wvm. dilg.gov.ph OPiNf0N 13 27 SEP This has reference to your letter dated 22 August 2016 seeking enlightenment on the following quoted queries: . In the approval of Appropriation Ordinances (Annual Budget), which type of "Majority" are we going to apply, the Simple Majority or the Qualified Majority rule? 2. Is my decision of disapproval by way of the result of' the voting right or valid? 3. In this case where the Appropriation Ordinance was already declared disapproved, is there any other way the SB Can do to pass the Appropriation Ordinance for the benefit and welfare of the constituents? 4. Is the Mun. Liga Vice President being endorsed and certified by the National Liga qualified to assume the position of the suspended Mun. Liga President in the Sangguniang Bayan despite being a losing candidate in the last national and local elections as stated in Section 94 of' the Local Government Code of 1991 ? In your letter, you represented the following: The elected vice-mayor of Vinzons, Camarines Norte during the last 09 May 2()16 Synchronized National and Local Elections, was dismissed by the Office of the Ombudsman. This gave way to your succession as the first-ranking sangguniang bayan member to the position of vice-mayor; Out of the remaining seven (7) elected sangguniang bayan members, three (3) of them were also suspended by the Office of the Ombudsman and are currently serving the same;

Clearly, Section 94 of the Local Government Code of 1991 is applicable only in cases of' appointment or designation of a losing candidate [o any office in the Government or any government-owned or controlled corporations or in any of their subsidiaries within one year 10110wing such election. The rationale behind the one-year prohibition is the extirpation of the "spoils system, " considering that the foregoing provisions are directed against the so-called "political lameducks'l Section 94 is not applicable in a situation that involves the application of the so- called rule of succession in elective office, which is by operation of law and therefore automatic. Please be reminded thal Lhe "vice-president of the municipal chapter of the LnB" is a Punong Barangay, thus an elective official. In this regard, may we invite your attention to the following: l. Section IO, Rule II of COMELEC Resolution No. 99842, promulgated on 18 August 2016--- "(A)nyperson holding an elective office or position shall not be considered resigned upen the filing ofa COC whether for the same or any other elective office or position. 2. Section 1, Article XII of the Constitution and By-Laws of the Liga ng mga Barangay, which states that the term of office of the liga officers shall be from the date of election until their successor have been elected and qualified, unless sooner removed from office for a cause. Based on the foregoing, when the vice-president of the Municipal Chapter of the LnB filed his certificate of candidacy for the position of Sangguniang Bayan Member vis- å-vis the 09 May 2016 Synchronized National and Local Elections, he is not considered to have abandoned his positions as Punong Barangay and as vice-president of the Municipal Chapter of the LnB. Furthermore, as you have represented, the National Executive Board of' the Liga ng Barangay have endorsed and gave certification that the vice-president of the LnB municipal chapter is qualified to succeed as president. Such endorsement and certification (b) Except for losing candidates in barangay elections, no candidate who lost in any election shall, within one (1) year after such election, be appointed to any office in the government or any government-owned or -controlled corporations or in any of their subsidiaries. Entitled "RULES AND REGULATIONS GOVERNING: 1) POLITICAL CONVENTIONS; 2) SUBMISSION OF NOMINEES OF GROUPS OR ORGANIZATIONS PARTICIPXI'ING UNDER THE PARTY-LIST SYSTEM OF REPRESENTATION; 3) FILING OF CERTIFICATES OF CANDIDACY AND NOMINATION OF AND ACCEPTANCE BY OFFICIAL CANDIDATES OF REGISTERED POLITICAL PARTIES OR COALITIONS OF POLITICAL PARTIES; AND 4) FILING OF ACCREDITATION FOR THE PURPOSE OF DETERMINING THE DOMINANT MAJORITY PAIWI'. DOMINANT MINORITY PARTY, TEN (10) MAJOR NATIONAL PARTIES AND TWO (2) MAJOR LOCAL PARTIES IN CONNECTION WITH THE MAY 9, 2016 NATIONAL AND LOCAL ELECI'IONS"

Jurisprudence defines "majority" as that which is 50% plus I or that which is greater than half of the membership of the body. We note, however, that using either formula will still give us the same result. Applying the foregoing in your case, please find hereunder our computation of a simple majority during that special session held on 18 August 2016: 5 2 2.5 3.5 — number of members present (including the Acting Vice-mayor) - It is noted however that there can never be a fractional member of a sanggunian. Hence, the more appropriate thing to do is to go down [o the next lower whole number, which is 3. Corollary thereto, if we take the relationship of 3 vis-å-vis 2.5, 3 is definitely greater than 2.5, hence, 3 is greater than lh of 5. Hence, it appears that the August Body composed of 5 members present had validly passed its Appropriation Ordinance. As to your fourth query, the vice-president of the municipal chapter of the LnB ran as Sangguniang Bayan Member in the recently concluded 09 May 2016 Synchronized National and Local Elections but lost. When the president of the LnB Municipal Chapter was suspended by the Office of the Ombudsman, the vice-president of the Municipal Chapter assumed as such and was in fact endorsed and certified qualified to assume by the LnB National F„xecutive Board. You are of the position that such assumption of the vice-president of the Municipal Chapter as president of the LnB Municipal Chapter is legally infirm citing Section 94 of the Local Government Code of 1991. We have a different view. Section 941 of the Local Government Code of 1991 finds no application on the case of the assumption of vice-president as President of the Municipal Chapter of the LnB in view of the temporary vacancy therein. SECTION 94. Appointment of Elective and Appointive Local Officials; Candidates Who Lost in an Election. — (a) No elective or appointive local official shall be eligible for appointment or designation in any capacity to any public office or position during his tenure. Unless otherwise allowed by law or by the primary functions of his position, no elective or appointive local official shall hold any other office or employment in the government or any subdivision, agency or instrumentality thereof, including governmentajwned or •controlled corporations or their subsidiaries;

The president of' the municipal chapter of the Liga ng mga Barangay (LnB) was also included among [hose suspended by the Office of the Ombudsman. Hence, he was replaced by [he vice-presiclent of the municipal chapter, who was endorsed by the National Executive Board of the Liga ng mga Barangay (1.1113) and the local DILG and has been recognized by the sangguniang bayan through a Resolution. Thereafter, the sangguniang bayan held a special session last 18 August 2016 attended by the following: You, as the Acting Vice-Mayor Four (4) Sangguniang Bayan Members One (l) SB Member was absent during that time. Given this composition, the August Body tried during that special session to approve the Appropriation Ordinance and arrived at a voting of three (3) in favor and one (l) against its approval. You, acting as the Presiding Officer during that special session, declared the Appropriation Ordinance as "disapproved" applying the formula in determining the majority of all the members: 50% + I majority such that 50% of 5 is 2.5 -v I — 3.5, rounding off the result would give us 4 which should be the majority. Hence, your subject let ter. We shall answer your first up to third queries in one (l) discussion since they are interrela ted. In this regard, please be informed that in DILG Legal Opinion No. 13, series of 2010, a simple majority is required to enact an Appropriation Ordinance and not a qualified majority. The applicable laws [Section 319 and 447 (2)(i) of Republic Act No. 7160 or the Local Government Code of 1991] are silent as to the kind of majority needed to pass an Appropriation Ordinance of' a municipality. The Department opined in DILG Legal Opinion No. 13, series of 2010 that "(I)t is 10 be noted that with respect to the enact,rnent of the annual and supplemental appropriations, the law is silent as to what kind of majority vote is required of that sanggunian, unlike succeeding provision which specifically require qualified majority vote of that sanggunian for their passage. In its silence, the presumption is, the same is to be enacted only by a simple majority vote. The difference between a simple majority and a qualified majority is that in the former, itis that which is the majority of the members present, there being a quorum. On the other hand, a qualified majority is one wherein the total membership of the sanggunian is considered in determining a majority.

